\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The even-half theorem of Jaffe and Pedrocchi}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Jaffe and Pedrocchi use an antilinear multiplicative
reflection on Majorana operators. Their self-adjoint
Hamiltonian decomposes into two even reflected half terms
and cross terms pairing a monomial with its reflection.
The cross coefficients are real and diagonal in that
monomial description, with the prescribed
#{\ui}-power determined by parity. Their sign condition
requires all odd-length cross coefficients to have one
common sign, while the even-length ones are nonpositive
\citet{(II.4)-(II.7), (III.1)-(III.8), pp. 3-4}{jaffepedrocchi2013reflection}.}

\p{Under these hypotheses, Theorem 3 proves positivity
of the trace of #{a\Theta(a)\ue^{-H}} for #{a} in the
even half-algebra, using ordinary multiplication.}

\p{In \citet{Theorem 3 and (VI.9), p. 6}{jaffepedrocchi2013reflection},
the one-generator odd example has the nonreal value
#{-2\ui\sinh(1)}.
Thus the even-half restriction is material. It does not
remove the cellular obstruction in \ref{fdip-00XY}, whose
negative element is already even and locally gauge
invariant. The source gives no identification of its
Majorana trace with this paired exterior coefficient.}
